1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,960
A hundred and forty-two points with two averages, two standard deviations, and a correlation of minus point oh six.

2
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,480
Picture the cloud.

3
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,950
Which way does its best-fit line tilt?

4
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:17,850
You probably pictured a shapeless oval, with a nearly flat line.

5
00:00:18,230 --> 00:00:19,710
Here are the real points.

6
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:25,430
A dinosaur, drawn by Alberto Cairo, with these same five numbers.

7
00:00:26,066 --> 00:00:34,786
In a review of seven hundred and three physiology papers, eighty-six percent used bar graphs: an average and an error bar.

8
00:00:35,166 --> 00:00:37,426
Only thirteen percent showed the points.

9
00:00:37,766 --> 00:00:39,906
So how much can five numbers hide?

10
00:00:40,286 --> 00:00:46,096
Let's hide a star in them, and demand that all five still match the dinosaur's to two decimals.

11
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,420
Version one slides every point onto its own spot on a star.

12
00:00:50,766 --> 00:00:53,696
We got the star, and lost all five numbers.

13
00:00:53,896 --> 00:00:59,206
The center moved, the x spread grew, and the y spread shrank by almost a quarter.

14
00:00:59,866 --> 00:01:02,556
So what exactly do five numbers pin down?

15
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:07,070
The two averages mark a single point: the cloud's balance point.

16
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:13,133
The two standard deviations reach one deviation out on each side, and draw a box.

17
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:15,623
Many ellipses touch all four walls.

18
00:01:15,823 --> 00:01:17,483
The correlation picks one.

19
00:01:17,863 --> 00:01:24,713
Correlation r means one standard deviation to the right, on average r standard deviations up.

20
00:01:25,093 --> 00:01:29,093
That's the best-fit line, through the middle of every vertical slice.

21
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:34,683
At the wall, the slice is just the touch point, r times ess why above the center.

22
00:01:35,063 --> 00:01:38,283
For minus point oh six, that's just below the middle.

23
00:01:38,633 --> 00:01:41,513
That ellipse is everything the five numbers know.

24
00:01:41,866 --> 00:01:44,786
Now replay version one, and watch the ellipse.

25
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:46,930
How would you fix the star?

26
00:01:48,766 --> 00:01:50,726
Slide it until the centers meet.

27
00:01:51,106 --> 00:01:53,226
Stretch it until the boxes match.

28
00:01:53,606 --> 00:01:58,196
Then tip it, a slight shear, until it touches the walls at the same height.

29
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:01,643
All five match now, to every decimal place.

30
00:02:02,300 --> 00:02:06,990
But add a sixth number, the median of y, and our star misses it by six.

31
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:14,523
In 2017, Justin Matejka and George Fitzmaurice built a search that keeps these five numbers fixed.

32
00:02:14,903 --> 00:02:20,053
It nudges a random point toward a target shape, and undoes any nudge that breaks them.

33
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:24,073
Repeat it enough times, and the dinosaur becomes a circle.

34
00:02:24,433 --> 00:02:27,723
From the dinosaur they grew twelve shapes, like this star,

35
00:02:28,723 --> 00:02:29,783
this bullseye,

36
00:02:30,583 --> 00:02:31,453
this X,

37
00:02:32,453 --> 00:02:33,503
these stripes,

38
00:02:34,303 --> 00:02:35,403
and these dots.

39
00:02:35,783 --> 00:02:39,383
Every one of them has this same ellipse, and a different median.

40
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:45,500
Francis Anscombe made the point in 1973, with eleven points of his own.

41
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:50,490
His correlation is point eight two, so the ellipse touches the right wall high up.

42
00:02:50,833 --> 00:02:58,643
His best-fit line follows the same rule: one ess ex across, r ess why up, through the center and that touch point.

43
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:00,873
So the slope is rise over run.

44
00:03:01,873 --> 00:03:04,153
Plug in his numbers, and it's one half.

45
00:03:04,766 --> 00:03:09,966
All four of his datasets share these five numbers, so they share the ellipse and the line.

46
00:03:10,266 --> 00:03:14,366
A trend, a curve, an outlier, and one point doing all the work.

47
00:03:15,833 --> 00:03:20,993
Your line was exactly right: five numbers fix its slope, minus point one.

48
00:03:21,373 --> 00:03:26,333
Your cloud was wrong: a blur around this ellipse, all that five numbers can draw.

49
00:03:26,933 --> 00:03:30,873
Most of those papers used a bar graph, which draws only two.

50
00:03:31,473 --> 00:03:34,173
The dinosaur lived in everything they leave out.

